(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 38-year-old man has yellow deposits in the creases of his palms (palmar xanthomas). His triglyceride is 410 mg/dL and total cholesterol 390 mg/dL. Genetic testing shows homozygosity for apolipoprotein E2. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Metabolic syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An apolipoprotein E mutation with apolipoprotein E2 homozygosity lets chylomicron and very-low-density remnants accumulate, raising triglyceride and total cholesterol both to 250 to 500, with distinctive palmar xanthomas. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which laboratory monitoring is listed for statin therapy?","choices":["Lipids, kidney function and potassium","Lipids, glucose and uric acid","Lipids, liver function and creatine kinase","Lipids, thyroid function and blood count"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The statin monitoring parameters are lipid levels, liver function and creatine kinase (also called creatine phosphokinase), checked alongside the repeat lipid panel. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has mitral stenosis found on an echocardiogram done before pregnancy. She has no symptoms and is in sinus rhythm. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Cardiology consultation","Start a loop diuretic","Balloon valvotomy now","No follow-up needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All patients with evidence of mitral stenosis on echocardiogram should have a cardiology consult to evaluate them and set the frequency of follow-up. Asymptomatic disease in sinus rhythm needs no therapy yet. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 43, 41"},{"q":"Which diuretics are potassium-wasting?","choices":["Dapagliflozin and empagliflozin","Lisinopril and irbesartan","Furosemide and hydrochlorothiazide","Spironolactone and eplerenone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics such as furosemide and bumetanide, and thiazide-like diuretics such as hydrochlorothiazide and metolazone, waste potassium. The mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists are potassium-sparing. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has an hour of substernal pressure. Her electrocardiogram shows ST-segment depression of 2 mm in V5 and V6 without ST elevation, and her standard troponin I is 0.62 ng/mL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Unstable angina","Infarction with ST elevation","Vasospastic angina","Infarction without ST elevation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ST-segment depression above 1 mm and/or T-wave inversion in two or more consecutive leads, with symptoms and an elevated troponin I (standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL), is a non-ST-elevation infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 47, 86; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has new swelling of both legs, heavy protein in her urine and low-density lipoprotein of 220 mg/dL. Before a statin is started, which condition should be ruled out?","choices":["Familial chylomicronemia","Heterozygous familial form","Nephrotic syndrome","Metabolic syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Elevated low-density lipoprotein calls for ruling out nephrotic syndrome, which drives excess very-low-density production and raises triglyceride and low-density lipoprotein. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 30%) has gained weight with leg edema and crackles. She takes a beta blocker, sacubitril-valsartan, spironolactone and dapagliflozin. Which drug relieves her fluid overload now?","choices":["A loop diuretic such as furosemide","Dobutamine given at home","Hydralazine given on its own","Diltiazem to slow the heart rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diuretics are used as needed for congestion in every ejection fraction group; loops such as furosemide and bumetanide are the potassium-wasting diuretics that clear fluid. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with diabetic autonomic neuropathy has recently begun fainting on standing. She has no supine hypertension (high pressure while lying flat), heart disease or kidney disease. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["A vasopressor infusion in clinic","Strict fluid restriction each day","Fludrocortisone before anything else","Slow position changes, treat the cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes and treatment of the underlying condition are first-line for orthostatic syncope; drugs come in when these are not enough. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has vomited for 2 days, has dry mucous membranes, and fainted on standing; her heart rate rises from 70 to 98 as her pressure falls 30 mmHg. Which is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Treat the underlying volume loss","Order a tilt-table test first","Start midodrine for autonomic failure","Refer her for an echocardiogram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her intact heart rate response and dryness point to volume depletion, and orthostasis is managed by treating the underlying condition. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein consists mostly of cholesterol and supplies cholesterol for cellular needs?","choices":["High-density lipoprotein","Chylomicrons","Low-density lipoprotein","Very-low-density lipoprotein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein consists mostly of cholesterol, is used to provide cholesterol for cellular needs, and its increased levels put patients at risk for atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has chest tightness the morning after a long-distance run that left his leg muscles sore. The team wants a blood marker specific to heart muscle. Which marker should be measured?","choices":["Any troponin type","Smooth-muscle troponin","Troponin C","Troponin I"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Troponins are proteins of cardiac and skeletal muscle, and there are three types (I, T and C), but troponin I is the one specific to the heart, so skeletal muscle injury from his run does not confound it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with worsening tricuspid regurgitation from pulmonary hypertension is being followed. Which symptoms mark that his regurgitation has become severe?","choices":["Hoarseness and dysphagia","Pedal edema and ascites","Exertional angina and syncope","Hemoptysis and orthopnea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mild to moderate tricuspid regurgitation is usually asymptomatic; severe disease causes pedal edema and ascites from right-sided congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a blood pressure of 224/126 with no headache, no visual change, no chest pain, a normal neurological examination and a normal creatinine. How should this be classified?","choices":["Hypertensive encephalopathy","A hypertensive emergency, because the systolic exceeds 220","Severe asymptomatic hypertension","Resistant hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An emergency is defined by acute hypertension-mediated ORGAN INJURY, not by crossing a numeric threshold. A very high pressure with no acute organ involvement is not an emergency, and that distinction decides how fast the pressure is brought down. It is not a hypertensive emergency. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has chest pain at rest with transient ST-segment elevation during the episode that resolves, and his coronary arteries show no high-grade stenosis. He smokes cigarettes and uses a recreational drug on weekends. Which substance should he be counseled to avoid because it can precipitate this condition?","choices":["Diltiazem","Cocaine","Nitroglycerin","Amlodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Drugs that may precipitate vasospastic angina include cocaine, ephedrine, marijuana, alcohol, amphetamine and triptans. It is relatively uncommon and often strikes those under 50. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 88, 89"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with exertional chest pain says he \"had a heart cath\" last week. The report shows dye was injected and the coronary arteries were imaged, revealing a stenosis, but nothing was done to the vessel. Which term names what was performed?","choices":["Plain old balloon angioplasty","Coronary angiography","Coronary artery bypass graft","Percutaneous coronary intervention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Coronary angiography is the injection of dye to identify a lesion or blockage, and it is the gold standard for diagnosing coronary artery disease. Nothing was done to the vessel, so no intervention took place. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 50"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man with obesity and type 2 diabetes has a triglyceride of 720 mg/dL, elevated total cholesterol, reduced high-density lipoprotein and a normal low-density lipoprotein. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Mixed dyslipidemia","Heterozygous familial form"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride above 500 with elevated total cholesterol and reduced high-density lipoprotein, usually without raised low-density lipoprotein, defines it, typically on a polygenic background with obesity or insulin resistance. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What causes neurogenic orthostatic syncope?","choices":["Autonomic nervous system dysfunction","Volume loss from diarrhea or bleeding","Emotional triggers raising vagal tone","An arrhythmia lowering cardiac output"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a sustained drop in pressure on standing or sitting upright caused by underlying damage or dysfunction in the autonomic nervous system. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with resistant hypertension is started on lisinopril. Her creatinine rises markedly within a week. What does this suggest?","choices":["Acute interstitial nephritis from the medication","Progression of underlying diabetic nephropathy","Expected drug effect requiring no change in approach","Bilateral renal artery stenosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Where perfusion depends on angiotensin-mediated efferent arteriolar tone, blocking it drops the filtration pressure sharply. This is one of the recognized clues to renovascular disease, and it is why the agent is contraindicated in bilateral stenosis. A hemodynamically significant unilateral stenosis does the same. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis with leg swelling and jugular venous distension. Before any procedure, which medical regimen is appropriate?","choices":["Warfarin with a heparin bridge","Low salt, diuretic, aldosterone antagonist","Nitrates, beta blocker, calcium blocker","Afterload reduction with hydralazine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis is managed with a low-salt diet, diuretics and an aldosterone antagonist, with bioprosthetic valve replacement preferred when the valve is replaced. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with a mechanical aortic valve on warfarin is scheduled for a colectomy that requires stopping it. Which plan should be avoided?","choices":["Bridging with heparin","Resuming a vitamin K antagonist","Rechecking his ratio after surgery","Stopping warfarin with no bridge"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If surgery requires interrupting warfarin, it must be bridged with heparin until the international normalized ratio is therapeutic; leaving a mechanical valve uncovered is the thing to avoid. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has progressive leg edema and jugular venous distension (distended neck veins). At the apex there is a low-pitched diastolic rumble with an opening snap. Which is the most likely cause of her right-sided heart failure?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Aortic valve stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Mitral valve stenosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A rumbling diastolic murmur at the apex with an opening snap is mitral stenosis, one of the three causes of right-sided failure with chronic left-sided failure and pulmonary arterial hypertension. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 14-year-old girl with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia remains severely elevated despite a high-intensity statin, ezetimibe and regular apheresis. Which adjunct is approved at her age?","choices":["Gemfibrozil","Evinacumab","Fish oil","Colesevelam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Evinacumab, an intravenous monoclonal antibody that lowers low-density lipoprotein and triglyceride, is adjunct therapy for patients 12 years and older with the homozygous disorder. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Why can nitroglycerin cause shock in a right ventricular infarct?","choices":["It provokes coronary spasm","It triggers major bleeding","It blocks the atrioventricular node","Lost preload drops output"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The infarcted right ventricle is preload dependent; decreasing preload reduces the Frank-Starling effect, the ventricle fails into right-sided heart failure, output falls and shock follows. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with a prior heart attack and reduced ejection fraction collapsed abruptly while walking, with no warning except a brief racing heartbeat. He is pale, his pressure is 86/52 and his heart rate is 150 and regular. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cardiogenic syncope from an arrhythmia","Situational reflex syncope","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Vasovagal reflex syncope"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Abrupt collapse with minimal prodrome after palpitations, in a patient with coronary disease and reduced ejection fraction, with a rapid regular rhythm, points to an arrhythmic cardiac cause. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with chronic mitral regurgitation has an echocardiogram showing a regurgitant fraction of 64%. Which severity does this indicate?","choices":["Moderate regurgitation","Ungradeable by echocardiogram","Mild regurgitation","Severe regurgitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral regurgitation is severe when the regurgitant fraction is 50% or more, meaning over half of the blood ejected goes backward. A fraction of 30 to 49% is moderate and below 30% is mild. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 50, 49"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has combined right and left heart failure with symptoms at rest despite optimized guideline-directed therapy. Which course is most appropriate to discuss?","choices":["Stopping all medicines and observing","Continuing dobutamine at home","Transplant evaluation or comfort care","Adding a calcium channel blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Combined right and left failure is managed with symptom control, comfort care and heart transplant. When optimized therapy no longer controls symptoms, those are the options to raise. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman with cardiomyopathy has had pressures around 92/58 for many months. She has intermittent lightheadedness that comes and goes. How is her hypotension best classified?","choices":["Chronic cardiogenic hypotension","Acute toxic hypotension","Long-lasting acute infectious hypotension","Acute reflex hypotension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cardiogenic causes such as cardiomyopathy and reduced ejection fraction heart failure produce chronic hypotension, which waxes and wanes and is intermittently symptomatic. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes has asymptomatic left ventricular hypertrophy on echocardiogram. She already takes an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. Which drug class should be added for her diabetes-related risk?","choices":["Calcium channel blocker such as amlodipine","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She is stage B, and stage B carries a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor for patients with diabetes as well as the converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with hypertension has weak delayed femoral pulses and a leg pressure lower than her arm pressure. Which study establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["A morning aldosterone to renin ratio","Polysomnography","Echocardiography","Renal duplex ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the referral that follows is to a vascular surgeon or a structural cardiologist. The bedside findings raise the question; the echocardiogram answers it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which are the earliest measurable markers of hypertensive kidney damage?","choices":["Hematuria and casts on urinalysis","Rising albuminuria and falling filtration rate","A rising serum potassium concentration","A further rise in the blood pressure itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and both are watched as trends rather than single values, because it is the direction of travel over time that identifies progression early enough to act on. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with no history of atherosclerotic disease asks whether she should start a statin for prevention. Her low-density lipoprotein is 145 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Calculate the Pooled Cohort risk","Automatic high-intensity statin","Clinical assessment and risk discussion","Refuse any statin due to age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Above 75 in primary prevention the approach is clinical assessment and a risk discussion rather than an automatic prescription. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 44-year-old newly diagnosed hypertensive patient asks why treatment matters if she feels well. What is the strongest answer?","choices":["Feeling well means her risk is already low","It causes cumulative organ damage with no reliable symptoms","She will begin to feel symptoms within a few months if untreated","Treatment is optional until organ damage is demonstrated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The heart, brain, kidneys, aorta, arteries and retina are all injured over years, and most patients have no reliable blood pressure-related symptoms to warn them. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man had a surgical valvuloplasty as a child to relieve right ventricular outflow obstruction. He now has a brief, high-pitched decrescendo early diastolic murmur at the second left intercostal space that radiates toward the mid right sternal border with full inspiration. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pulmonic stenosis","Tricuspid stenosis","Pulmonic regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A brief, high-pitched early diastolic murmur at the second left intercostal space with inspiratory radiation (the Graham Steell murmur) is pulmonic regurgitation, and surgery on the right ventricular outflow tract is its most common cause. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 63, 61"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man whose brother had a heart attack at 50 has lipoprotein(a) measured at 85 mg/dL. His low-density lipoprotein is 140 mg/dL. What does the lipoprotein(a) result mean for his management?","choices":["He needs aggressive low-density lowering","It changes nothing in his plan","He needs a fibrate for the value","It is protective and lowers his risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Elevated lipoprotein(a) is a causal and independent risk factor for atherosclerotic disease, and a patient with it needs aggressive therapy to decrease low-density lipoprotein. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with reduced ejection fraction is started on spironolactone alongside her loop diuretic. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["It replaces the need for her beta blocker","It spares potassium, so levels are checked","It wastes potassium like her loop diuretic","It is only a short course for congestion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone is a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, a potassium-sparing diuretic, which is why her potassium and renal function will be followed alongside the potassium-wasting loop. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which heart sound belongs to diastolic heart failure?","choices":["A pericardial rub","An opening snap","Fourth heart sound","Third heart sound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A fourth heart sound is strong left atrial contraction against a stiff, hypertrophied left ventricle, and it sits in the diastolic chain with hypertension and edema that starts peripherally. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is three days out from a myocardial infarction complicated by acute heart failure, with an ejection fraction of 30%. A colleague proposes an implantable defibrillator before discharge. Which step is premature at this point?","choices":["Optimizing her guideline therapy","Implanting a defibrillator now","Starting a wearable defibrillator","Referring to cardiac rehabilitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After acute heart failure from infarction, the pathway is a wearable defibrillator, optimized therapy and cardiac rehabilitation, then a repeat echocardiogram; the implantable device follows only if there is no improvement. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 58-year-old hypertensive man has abrupt severe tearing chest pain radiating to his back, maximal at onset. His right arm pressure is 176/92 and his left is 138/78. A chest radiograph is reported as normal. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Delay imaging until the blood pressure is controlled","Reassure him, since the chest radiograph is normal","Obtain an echocardiogram to establish the diagnosis","Obtain immediate cross-sectional vascular imaging"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A normal chest radiograph does NOT exclude dissection, so a normal film must not end the workup. The pain that is maximal at onset and the asymmetric arm pressures both point to it, and surgical involvement is urgent. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has microvascular angina confirmed after angiography showed normal coronary arteries. Which treatment is appropriate?","choices":["Thrombolytic therapy","Calcium channel blocker","Reassurance alone","Coronary stent placement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of microvascular angina is very similar to coronary artery disease: lifestyle modification and risk-factor reduction, a calcium channel blocker, beta blocker, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and statin. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with mildly reduced ejection fraction (fraction 44%) has ankle edema and breathlessness. Which therapy carries the strongest recommendation?","choices":["A diuretic as needed","A neprilysin combination drug","An evidence-based beta blocker","A mineralocorticoid antagonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In mildly reduced ejection fraction, diuretics as needed carry the one strong recommendation. The sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor is moderate; the beta blocker, antagonist and renin-angiotensin drugs are weak. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with Marfan syndrome has a high-pitched blowing diastolic murmur at the third intercostal space at the left upper sternal border that radiates to the apex. He has bounding pulses and a wide pulse pressure. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pulmonic regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blowing means regurgitation and diastolic narrows it to the aortic or pulmonic valve; the third intercostal space with radiation to the apex, bounding pulses and a wide pulse pressure mark aortic regurgitation. Marfan syndrome is a root-disease cause. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 27, 28"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman in a clinic has hypotension from a suspected overwhelming infection and is awaiting transfer. Which therapy should not be started in the clinic?","choices":["Reviewing her medications","Arranging urgent transfer","A norepinephrine infusion","Repeating her vital signs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, dopamine, phenylephrine and vasopressin are listed for the emergency department and intensive care only. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 59-year-old hypertensive man is breathless with bibasal crackles and raised jugular venous pressure. Is chest radiography appropriate?","choices":["Yes, because findings suggest pulmonary edema","No, because a normal film would exclude dissection","No, because chest radiography is never indicated in hypertension","Yes, as a routine part of every hypertensive workup"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Radiography is appropriate when dyspnea or findings suggest pulmonary edema, when cardiomegaly is suspected, or when a widened mediastinum is a concern. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman's mother had a myocardial infarction at 70 and her brother had one at 50. She asks which relative's heart attack raises her own coronary risk. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Both relatives'","Only her brother's","Neither relative's","Only her mother's"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Family history counts when a male first-degree relative had sudden death or myocardial infarction before 55, or a female first-degree relative before 65. Her brother at 50 qualifies; her mother at 70 does not. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the commonest cause to exclude before labeling hypertension as resistant?","choices":["Undiagnosed primary aldosteronism","Incorrect cuff size","Excess alcohol intake","Medication nonadherence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it heads a list that also includes measurement error, white coat effect, high sodium or alcohol intake, interfering drugs, and undiagnosed sleep apnea or secondary hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which auscultatory finding is typical of mitral valve prolapse?","choices":["An opening snap after the second sound","A blowing diastolic murmur to the apex","A mid-systolic click at the apex","A diastolic rumble near the xiphoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse gives a mid-systolic click at the apex. It is usually benign and asymptomatic, but it may develop the symptoms and signs of mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which maneuver makes the aortic stenosis murmur louder?","choices":["Squatting","Handgrip","Standing","Valsalva"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Squatting, like sitting forward, raises preload and left ventricular volume, so more blood crosses the aortic valve and the murmur grows louder; sitting forward also brings the valve closer to the chest wall. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with heart failure has cool extremities, a narrow pulse pressure and increasing confusion. His urine output has fallen and creatinine has doubled. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Acute kidney injury from low perfusion","Urinary retention from prostatic disease","Nephrotic syndrome from proteinuria","Hypertensive kidney damage from years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cool extremities, narrow pulse pressure and obtundation are signs of low perfusion, and renal dysfunction with acute kidney injury and falling urine output is part of the same picture. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 20-year-old woman has a classic history of palpitations and lightheadedness on standing that improve lying down, but her tilt-table test is negative. How should the negative result be interpreted?","choices":["It does not exclude the syndrome","It points to a cardiac cause","It points to autonomic failure","It rules the syndrome out"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With a sensitivity of about 40%, many patients with the syndrome test negative, so a negative result tells almost nothing; a positive result, with specificity above 80%, carries more weight. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman with very heavy menstrual bleeding has fatigue, palpitations, ankle edema and a raised B-type natriuretic peptide. Echocardiogram is being arranged. Which additional test is most likely to identify the driver of her heart failure?","choices":["Complete blood count","Chest radiograph","Cardiac troponin level","Right heart catheterization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anemia is one of the causes of high-output failure, and the complete blood count is part of the high-output workup with the echocardiogram, metabolic panel and natriuretic peptide. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is recovering from an ST-elevation myocardial infarction. Several weeks later he develops pleuritic chest pain and a pericardial friction rub. Which complication of the infarction does this represent?","choices":["Myocardial rupture","Thromboembolism","Pericarditis","Ventricular aneurysm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis (Dressler syndrome) is listed among the mechanical complications of myocardial infarction. Pleuritic pain with a friction rub weeks after the infarct points to the pericardium rather than the ventricle. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has breathlessness, leg edema and a B-type natriuretic peptide of 280. Which study should come next to define his cardiac structure and ejection fraction?","choices":["Cardiac troponin level","Echocardiogram","Repeat the peptide in a year","Right heart catheterization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An indeterminate peptide leaves the question open, and the echocardiogram shows structure most accurately and classifies heart failure by ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which description defines syncope?","choices":["Collapse with confusion lasting an hour","Dizziness and tunnel vision without collapse","Prolonged unconsciousness needing support","Brief loss of consciousness, then full recovery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Syncope is a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness with inability to maintain postural tone, followed by spontaneous recovery. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with hypertension has heart failure with a reduced ejection fraction and a myocardial infarction six months ago. Which class is preferred for him?","choices":["Clonidine","A beta blocker","Hydralazine as monotherapy","A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are not first-line for uncomplicated hypertension, but reduced ejection fraction heart failure, recent infarction and ischemic disease are exactly the comorbidities that select them. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has fatigue and breathlessness with exertion. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 45%, and her fraction has never been lower. Which heart failure group describes her?","choices":["Preserved ejection fraction","Reduced ejection fraction","Improved ejection fraction","Mildly reduced ejection fraction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. 45% falls in the 41 to 49% band, and with no earlier value of 40% or less there is nothing to make it improved ejection fraction. Her treatment then resembles preserved ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 48-year-old Black man develops lip and tongue swelling three months after starting an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. What should be known about this?","choices":["An angiotensin receptor blocker carries no risk whatsoever","It is a dose-related effect that resolves on dose reduction","A beta blocker is the appropriate substitution","It is two to four times more common in this group"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a bradykinin-mediated class effect of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibition. Angiotensin receptor blockers can usually be used instead, though angioedema with them remains possible. An angiotensin receptor blocker is usually substituted. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has exertional breathlessness. Her electrocardiogram shows tall R waves in V5 and V6 and deep S waves in V1 and V2. What does this pattern show?","choices":["Right heart strain","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Acute inferior injury","A normal tracing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tall R waves in V5 and V6 with deep S waves in V1 and V2 indicate left ventricular hypertrophy, the structural change behind hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and the diastolic chain. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with borderline-high low-density lipoprotein asks which foods can help lower his cholesterol. Which dietary advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Add soluble fiber and plant sterols","Eat more refined carbohydrate","Cut out all vegetables","Add more saturated and trans fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Therapeutic lifestyle changes include less saturated fat, more soluble fiber and plant stanols or sterols, cholesterol-lowering substances found in grains, vegetables, fruits, nuts and seeds. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which description fits typical cardiac chest pain?","choices":["Sharp pain that shifts with position","Burning pain relieved by exertion","Pain reproduced by pressing the chest","Substernal pressure with exertion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Typical cardiac chest pain is substernal chest pressure, worse with exertion, radiating to the neck or shoulder, with shortness of breath and diaphoresis, relieved by nitroglycerin. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 27"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has fainted twice, each time with warmth and nausea beforehand and quick recovery. Which part of his evaluation is most useful in establishing the diagnosis?","choices":["A tilt-table test","A stress test","The history","An echocardiogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important portion of diagnosing reflex syncope, summed up as history, history, history, supported by a log of episodes. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"}]};
